\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186262996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d9bf12-1fe7-50dc-9578-4733a58fcc41" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186260187} {:answer/id #uuid "5e80cdde-f67a-52d1-b9e6-283a9e5a6e6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186260687} {:answer/id #uuid "904deeb8-4993-5778-8122-16156e6f6561" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186261130} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a5450b-908b-530a-9257-8e2aed75f4e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186261327} {:answer/id #uuid "ae00115d-20ff-5d8c-b6f5-cece2308c9f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186261407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d9bf12-1fe7-50dc-9578-4733a58fcc41" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186260187} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e732691-405c-563a-9b89-29904e1c087a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ce5c5ca-b382-5ac8-8df3-b2e87f07de7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129232" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since ${a}_{33}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the third row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{33}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186262997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047b9c4d-753c-53d6-bc02-75b4865a21c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186259915} {:answer/id #uuid "2b66e2c3-c7ed-5ddc-ab29-939040202f4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186260258} {:answer/id #uuid "623f6e9a-04e3-5665-bc68-fc2a6b886cdd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186260724} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd9aaee-2bd6-57c9-997c-bcd1e81412b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186260833} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ecdabb-c846-5027-974d-168d140bb5fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186261786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047b9c4d-753c-53d6-bc02-75b4865a21c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186259915} :answer-field/id #uuid "25a2dccd-cf8a-5ad2-9c5d-6de8d5d13d8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "842b19e0-4a35-57fa-95ff-d59ed695ed85" :question/math-academy-id "q-129233" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the first row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186265721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dae3978-5fb1-5677-b02c-62d2326ec766" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264211} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff1ff22-5359-5e91-b43f-8349426c9d94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264233} {:answer/id #uuid "c43ce85f-eb8a-55cf-a2cb-92faafc201ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264715} {:answer/id #uuid "e24ff610-4726-5841-8d7e-3ff68d333f74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186264964} {:answer/id #uuid "fd96bf58-c997-5f12-8521-10736a62d606" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186265229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24ff610-4726-5841-8d7e-3ff68d333f74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186264964} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebfbaea-278e-5275-9910-a2f3b87e9425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32eaab66-626d-536b-9465-8d972be98972" :question/math-academy-id "q-129405" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186265765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf8d0c2-b55b-54c8-80e6-218235c3d5cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263379} {:answer/id #uuid "4e97e4a9-6185-5e4d-910d-c538b1cb2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263682} {:answer/id #uuid "98309dde-974d-523a-9d07-628f7f8605d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264308} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e6e5a3-e7aa-5e4c-885b-491a4f477364" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186265160} {:answer/id #uuid "f806378f-86bd-5e52-a34e-5e4c3e4a0105" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e6e5a3-e7aa-5e4c-885b-491a4f477364" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186265160} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f91e8d-5ee1-5973-b445-d68045b24cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16d77304-e003-57b3-942e-c991453c4142" :question/math-academy-id "q-129658" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d744e19-cc0b-54b5-b989-f42879524e2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263269} {:answer/id #uuid "3d85de02-55a0-5ca9-a16a-8296964b04cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263547} {:answer/id #uuid "641a3d60-f81d-5a15-b7e6-db675b854a22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263866} {:answer/id #uuid "750a4b1f-e819-5549-a27c-cb6830eae249" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264016} {:answer/id #uuid "eb154c09-133f-5eed-9b6b-7f97f2c82d4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d85de02-55a0-5ca9-a16a-8296964b04cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263547} :answer-field/id #uuid "380912f3-948e-5fe5-9b00-4f1bb0537b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60b29014-e789-5b3b-ac4b-a01d242582dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-129662" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-4,4]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a8671db243a19fb258f6144d6aba1b193e237ef93d45d877829f80b93bc7d36.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1fd765-1f38-5253-821b-f92c7d7a22ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263256} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7dcc02-00ba-51d0-a9b3-959e95073658" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263538} {:answer/id #uuid "85e25141-78e8-5a2d-a476-0226e7236451" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264155} {:answer/id #uuid "965c458a-34a0-5436-ac45-8e51732bdd28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264285} {:answer/id #uuid "d53144bc-77d0-5f00-885f-be170ba5f88f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d53144bc-77d0-5f00-885f-be170ba5f88f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264854} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb0b24bd-c34c-59e1-9c5b-f4578cccaaa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a14dc47-818c-583a-957a-79ea6806459b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129666" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,1]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/78/78af77d697ea292385e2bd2570c8185c728f57cc70c3edcc25ecccd0e84780e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles II and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle I cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c8a2ef-b27c-5471-aea9-5adb880ca8c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263437} {:answer/id #uuid "3f81b200-fc07-5472-814d-223d85c3a5e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263561} {:answer/id #uuid "871102bb-7974-5528-9d79-748024a9114e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264170} {:answer/id #uuid "a367f924-7bd7-54c7-a474-a455c852e0f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264387} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8daf6f-e5a8-56d4-a979-fc3c424d18e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca8daf6f-e5a8-56d4-a979-fc3c424d18e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264770} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e06b4b-2064-516f-b6c0-8b2bc90ec1f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9794411d-a692-56fa-886b-52950afee48e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129783" :question/problem "![](images/77/779f39d9b8eca56cf8b7b2b9355364cd95171ca61c3e07cabd69e7a048933e22.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,3]$\n- ${∬}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "649c18e6-b335-57b8-b91f-4a3b4fb8740b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263871} {:answer/id #uuid "9383bbac-0a12-574f-bdb2-ba13fc4cdf44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264263} {:answer/id #uuid "c7360bba-a115-5e8c-82e1-07ee1f227f6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264743} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc7eebe-96db-5195-b572-0f33685ae924" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264912} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d74fcf-91aa-54d7-a493-fe2f7cf73e9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9383bbac-0a12-574f-bdb2-ba13fc4cdf44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264263} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc9b1cb3-e115-5501-bbbe-215eca95063e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "827c070f-3c