X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ | $0.8$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.64.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.8\\cdot 0.8=0.64.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =0.16=0.2\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.01\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.1={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bb620a-f093-5caa-b1a9-20fc5f8cfee9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186272687} {:answer/id #uuid "80870de8-5e3b-5b1b-9028-7729c2eaea11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273489} {:answer/id #uuid "8c12813a-38d4-579d-a916-788bd5895610" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186273595} {:answer/id #uuid "91d2a50b-e57c-5a57-b276-1c5c736e21b3" :answer/type {:db/id 175