in that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.05.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.3\\cdot 0.65=0.195.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c4360e-11aa-50a5-8dca-4dea8a8fdd3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id 17592186272992} {:answer/id #uuid "892b0517-6002-5679-8747-3e18957a3c43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186273568} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d73e2e-3487-5a60-a79c-f7913c20e904" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186274069} {:answer/id #uuid "ca570e3b-a84a-5db7-a59c-54a705149244" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186274144} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f2b8dc-2166-5acf-96d8-0e209e971eb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186274221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892b0517-6002-5679-8747-3e18957a3c43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186273568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8185736-7186-5fec-abfc-3ab0eb208611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186275070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2475e8ae-4517-52f5-bd41-fe3d91352945" :question/math-academy-id "q-143680" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89bddea6-bbe2-51b8-adf4-de306f1c6836" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273576} {:answer/id #uuid "8e52a1ab-35d5-5c3f-8591-929097128649" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273617} {:answer/id #uuid "94a7a522-7254-5d79-914a-36556eb0b65c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273682} {:answer/id #uuid "b22de866-0abf-5b09-a709-899483e618d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273926} {:answ